WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.970
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Welcome,
and thanks for joining us.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.477
Today we're going to be
talking about how Google Cloud

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:14.060
Observability suite of products
is creating a better developer

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.250
experience for its customers.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.172
I'm Bryan Zimmerman, product
manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:19.172 --> 00:00:21.380
MEREDITH HASSETT: And I'm
Meredith Hassett, developer

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.297
advocate at Corvid by Wix.

00:00:24.297 --> 00:00:25.880
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: As
I mentioned, we're

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.547
going to be talking
about specific tools

00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:30.100
that help developers be
more successful day to day.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.750
But these exist within
a larger ecosystem.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.240
And this toolset is based off
the same planet scale core

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.750
platform that powers Google's
internal observability.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.210
So you know it's
been battle-tested.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.170
In addition, it comes with
built-in zero day observability

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.490
for key Google services.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.990
This is all under the umbrella
of our logging and monitoring

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.820
products, but it
includes much more.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.530
When talking about how to
help developers day to day,

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.450
we first have to ask,
what do developers

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.540
needs from their
observability toolset?

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.040
When we talk to our
customers, there

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.590
are three key
themes that we see.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.510
First, they need to
diagnose issues quickly.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.910
When there's an issue with
your application or site,

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.900
there isn't time to go
hunting for logs or hunting

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:18.680
for metrics.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.540
You need to be able
to find the problem

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.880
and diagnose the
problem with ease.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.050
Second, some problems are
harder to find than others.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.120
And with a large data available
about your application

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.548
and the infrastructure it runs
on-- especially in the cloud--

00:01:33.548 --> 00:01:35.340
having a toolset that
helps you effectively

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.330
find needles in that
haystack is really important.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.380
Thirdly, not all issues
can be reproduced

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.650
in a development environment.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.310
And even if they can
be, it's time consuming

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.440
and sometimes you
need answers quickly.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.720
So being able to
debug in production

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.470
is often critical to reducing
mean time to resolution

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.330
and being effective
in the cloud.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.650
We're going to be talking
about each of these areas,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.130
with specific examples of
how they can help developers

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.729
be more effective.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.650
First of all, let's talk about
diagnosing issues quickly.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.290
Metrics are a key part
to understanding problems

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.570
and what's happening
with your system.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.760
Traces in logs have
the context and details

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.410
to help you diagnose the
cause of said issues,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.630
but they're not always
linked together.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.470
So going from a metric to
the request and logs that

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.830
generated that particular
metric data point

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.680
is difficult and important
to be able to diagnose issues

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.910
quickly.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.650
What's the solution?

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.740
A feature we call "example
traces," otherwise known

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.953
as trace exemplars.

00:02:45.953 --> 00:02:47.370
In the screenshot
on the left, you

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.230
can see a heat map
distribution of latency.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.450
That has two
important additions--

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.910
one, there are aggregate
percentile lines

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:59.320
that help us understand how
this distribution is trending.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.290
Second, there are example
distributed traces

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:03.670
that have been sampled.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.337
These represent
complete requests

00:03:06.337 --> 00:03:08.170
and you can actually
drill into that request

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.662
without leaving the context
of your monitoring tool, which

00:03:10.662 --> 00:03:11.620
you'll see in a moment.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.040
Now let's see this in action.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.610
The demo we're about to show you
is of our example application

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.770
called Online Boutique.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.080
In this scenario, users
of the application,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.740
who normally experience
great performance,

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:31.593
have started to notice
extremely degraded latency.

00:03:31.593 --> 00:03:33.260
The developer in the
video will show you

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.270
how to use the Google
Cloud operations Tool

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:39.070
Suite and example traces,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.960
to quickly diagnose said issue.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.530
Here we will show how we can
use Cloud Monitoring and Cloud

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.630
Trace to quickly
understand where

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.090
we should be focusing our
attention with regards

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.120
to a particular issue.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.700
In this case, we
have an alert related

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.940
to 99th percentile
latency, which has spiked

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.000
quite significantly recently.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.280
Drilling into our
performance dashboard,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.480
we can see that there was a
significant increase in latency

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.300
around the time of the alert.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.810
This seems to possibly
correlate with error rate,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:22.630
but not exactly.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.360
This increase can be shown
both by the distribution

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.030
itself, but also the
aggregate percentile lines

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.370
and how they have
increased over time.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.350
Let's drill in further
to the inflection point.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.420
Now, typically, this is
actually quite difficult

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.900
to go from here.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.420
Understanding the
specific requests

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:45.920
that led to that
metric data point

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.010
is not always easily available.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.690
However, with Google
Cloud Operations,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.550
we can overlay example traces
onto this metrics graph.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.830
Now we can easily,
without switching context,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.020
drill into exactly
what was happening

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.182
at the time the
issue was observed.

00:05:03.182 --> 00:05:04.640
So in this instance,
this is a call

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.810
to the product service, which
is making a call to get product,

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.720
and making further calls
to get product, which

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.610
is an interesting behavior.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.650
Out of the 7 seconds
duration, about 6.9 of them

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.500
were related to get product.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.000
Let's see if this
seems to correlate

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.830
with other types of requests.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.440
This is another similar
such product call,

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.030
which has a similar
profile of child calls

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.480
to the product catalog service.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.180
This here is a cart operation,
so that's different.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.340
However, when we
look further, it

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.990
has this same get repeated get
product call in the product

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:48.715
catalog service
that we saw earlier.

00:05:53.502 --> 00:05:54.960
And if we look
further, we're going

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.650
to see similar behavior
of product catalog service

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:05.020
operations representing the
majority of the latency.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.430
Now, to be sure of this,
let's compare with traces

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:10.930
prior to the issue.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.260
Here's a similar
get product call.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:18.310
And it's making calls to the
recommendation service, again,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.470
to the get product service.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.230
And it makes a few
calls to get product,

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.900
which is what we saw before.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.950
However, if you look
at the total latency,

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:33.790
only 31 milliseconds were spent
within the get product service.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:36.520
This compares to 20 seconds in
some of the other examples we

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.904
were investigating.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:45.170
So it definitely seems to be
a delta in the length of time

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:49.220
of get product calls that is
representative of this latency.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.430
At this point, I have
a viable hypothesis

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.200
for what caused this issue.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.240
And I got there without
actually leaving

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.710
my monitoring dashboard.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.910
From here, I would look at
logging, perhaps debugging,

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.240
error reporting, to try and
understand what was actually

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.050
causing this issue.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.980
But what we've shown here is
that with these integrated

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.160
tools, you can quickly
understand where you should

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.080
be focusing your
attention and where

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.580
the problem may be located.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.420
The previous example
focused on speed.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.020
But that's not
always what you need.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.540
When it comes to finding
needles in the haystack,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.970
it's about breadth, depth, and
thoroughness of your tool set.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:37.120
In the next example,
we're going to show

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.430
how you can use the Google
Cloud Operations Tool

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.940
Suite to look for a very
difficult-to-find cause

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:45.000
of an issue.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.990
This focus is on
logging and trace.

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:51.180
And we'll follow that
up with profiler.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.660
To demonstrate finding
a needle in a haystack,

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:58.040
we're going to look through
Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Trace,

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.323
and Cloud Logging.

00:08:00.323 --> 00:08:01.740
Starting with Cloud
Monitoring, we

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.980
see that we have an
alert related to latency.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.510
Looking at our dashboards,
in specific, the performance

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:13.520
dashboard, we see
that there actually

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.610
was a spike in the errors
on the front end that

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.170
occurred fairly recently.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.410
To troubleshoot
this, we're going

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:24.540
to drill into things
with Cloud Logging.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.010
Looking in the logs
viewer, I can very easily

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:31.940
filter for the log lines
that contain error.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:35.434
I can also drill down to
the Kubernetes resource type

00:08:35.434 --> 00:08:36.559
where the errors came from.

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:40.010
In this case, they're almost
all from the front end.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.799
So what's happening
in these instances?

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:44.480
Drilling into the
details for these

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.370
logs, I can see
what's happening.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:51.230
In this case, I see fail
to get recommendations.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:55.910
I expect that other
errors are very similar.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.920
Failed to get product
recommendations again.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.230
I can add this to
the summary line

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:07.260
in order to see how prevalent
this error is within my filter

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:07.760
criteria.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.880
Now, to understand what the
recommendation service is

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.040
doing, let's open Cloud Trace.

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:26.108
And in this case, I'm
going to filter for spans--

00:09:26.108 --> 00:09:27.525
otherwise known
as service calls--

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.400
where my front end is calling
my recommendation service.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:46.310
We can see that the latency
spiked heavily around the time

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.370
that I received the alert and
started receiving those errors.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:55.030
Let's see what's happening.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.460
What we can see here is after
calling list recommendations,

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.830
the system is making
multiple calls

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:03.848
to get product from the
product catalog service.

00:10:03.848 --> 00:10:04.640
That's interesting.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:07.370
In fact, in this
19 second trace,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.490
which started as a
get cart request,

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.010
almost all of the latency
is spent within those

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.110
get product calls.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.990
That's definitely not
what we want to see.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.782
And it suggests that that's
where the issue is occurring.

00:10:23.253 --> 00:10:25.170
We're going to use Cloud
Logging to understand

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.730
what's happening within that
product catalog service.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:31.620
Again, it's very
easy to find this out

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.290
by drilling in to the resource
type in the field explorer.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.590
We're going to filter for
the pod named product catalog

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.480
service.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.990
Now, what we can see is roughly
at the time of the error,

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.570
there is a massive spike
in logs from this service.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.600
Narrowing down to
this time interval,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:56.240
we can see that almost
all of these logs

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.470
are a successfully
parsed product catalog.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.750
At this point, it's safe to
assume that this is where

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:05.030
I should focus my attention.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:07.400
I'm going to open up my
IDE and understand what's

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.850
happening with the parsing
of the product catalog

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:14.040
to find out why is it
actually successfully parsing

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:17.060
the product catalog so often.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.610
This is a good example of
how to use the operations

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:24.080
toolset, including
logging, trace, monitoring,

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:30.290
and more to quickly narrow
down a hard-to-find problem.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.720
Sometimes a problem
can't be easily found

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.887
in the logs or the traces.

00:11:34.887 --> 00:11:36.470
It has to do more
with the functioning

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:42.210
of your running code, or perhaps
it's about cost optimization.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:46.010
This is where a profiler
is extremely useful.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:48.670
Kellyanne is going to show
you how you can use Profile

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.420
to solve these
needle-in-the-haystack type

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.220
problems.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.170
KELLYANNE: Log Profiler can
help developers understand

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:59.490
how their services are spending
compute and memory resources.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.770
We will explore
how Profiler can be

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:04.920
used to identify
optimization opportunities

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:08.140
and help you run your
services more efficiently.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.440
Profiler supports
four languages--

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.500
Java, Go, NodeJS, and Python.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:17.100
For this walk through, we will
use the product catalog service

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.830
and its CPU time profile tag.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.520
The default view for provider
is the flame graph view

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:24.750
shown here.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:28.650
It helps developers understand
which portions of their code

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.900
are using the most resources.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:34.770
In this service, we can see
that the stack with the function

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:38.750
parse catalog is using
the most CPU time--

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:39.790
about 72%.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:46.540
We can see the parse catalog is
calling the JSON and Marshall

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:48.380
function.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:53.790
So this has to be the first
focus of our investigation.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.570
Now, let's open the
top Functions table

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:02.390
and sort the Functions
list by the Serve Time.

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:07.970
We can see that 8.3% of CPU time
is spent in the function state

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:13.670
in string, which is not evident
from the default view shown

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:16.370
in the flame graph.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:18.785
By clicking on
this function name,

00:13:18.785 --> 00:13:23.360
we load the flame graph
in the focus filter mode.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.000
This more is useful
to identify functions,

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.140
which are called from multiple
locations in your code.

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:35.180
It also helps us understand
the various calling paths

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.430
into this function.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.740
This is very useful for
understanding the resource

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:42.830
usage patterns of
library functions, which

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:48.160
may be called from different
locations in your code.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.740
Removing the focus filter
resets the flame graph

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.750
to its default view.

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.990
Now let's examine how we
can use the wait filter

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:58.540
to identify profiles
within a selected

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:01.190
percentage of peak consumption.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:05.560
For example, top 10% indicates
that only profiles that

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:09.010
were collected during the
top 10% of CPU consumption

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.070
are presented for analysis.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:17.620
We can also use the compare to
function to compare profiles

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:20.690
from two different wait groups.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.950
Here we are comparing
profiles from the top 10%

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:27.410
with all profiles.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:32.120
The frames that are
shaded in orange

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.640
have higher resource
consumption when

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:37.100
compared with all profiles.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.260
We can see that there is
some increase in resource

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:44.180
consumption in the client
handshake function stack,

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:46.100
as well.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:52.620
Resetting the compared to loads
the default flame graph view.

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:55.310
So these are some of
the features of Profiler

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.150
that can be used by
developers to understand

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:02.540
how their services
are spending resources

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:06.560
and where they should start
their optimization efforts.

00:15:09.272 --> 00:15:10.980
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Our
final scenario talks

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.370
about how to debug
code when it counts.

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.860
If you've ever had a
scenario where you could not

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:20.020
reproduce an issue in your
development environment,

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:21.570
then this is for you.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:25.290
If you've ever had a scenario
where that one line of logging

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:28.660
that you needed was not
there when you needed it,

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:30.780
this is for you.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.400
In this next demo,
we're going to see

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:37.693
how Cloud Debugger can help
you debug in production

00:15:37.693 --> 00:15:38.610
when you need it most.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:46.090
To start this journey, we're
going to open error reporting.

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:47.950
Error reporting
automatically surfaces

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:52.120
errors found in the logs
for easy discoverability.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:54.730
Within the error group,
we can see the trend

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:58.060
where this error has
occurred, where in the code

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:00.520
this error has
occurred, and easily

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.680
link to the logs that
generated the error.

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:09.160
Exploring these logs,
we can see quite easily.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:11.610
But this is exclusively caused
by the currency service.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:17.565
We can inspect the logs
for further details,

00:16:17.565 --> 00:16:18.690
including resource context.

00:16:21.937 --> 00:16:23.520
However, this doesn't
actually show us

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:26.640
what's happening and causing
this particular problem.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.010
For that, we're going
to open Cloud Debugger.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.060
Debugger allows
us to see what is

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:36.702
happening in our actual
running production code.

00:16:36.702 --> 00:16:38.160
In the logs, you
saw that the error

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:44.340
was happening at line 55 of
the currency service server JS.

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.650
Clicking here, we
can take a snapshot

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:50.190
of what's happening at the
code at that exact position,

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.150
inspecting the details
of the variables.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:54.330
This was done without
restarting the service,

00:16:54.330 --> 00:17:00.700
without pausing the service,
completely safe in production.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:02.580
Now, the cause
isn't obvious here,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.920
but we can see from the
code that if results

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.930
dot units is not
greater than zero,

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.740
it would throw this error.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.685
So let's see what results
dot units is set at.

00:17:12.685 --> 00:17:15.990
In this case, it's
set at not a number.

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.150
So that's obviously
not going to work.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.030
Let's see how this
was actually set.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:29.910
And in this case,
where we set that value

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.862
was also not a valid number.

00:17:32.862 --> 00:17:34.320
As such, it will
generate the error

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:37.230
that you saw today in the logs.

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.365
Next, we would need to update
the code to resolve this issue.

00:17:40.365 --> 00:17:42.240
But you can see how with
production debugging

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:47.640
this was easy to find without
any impact to my system.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:50.620
And that concludes
our three scenarios.

